F: the most levels a fresh process reads (a process per trial). W: the most a process reads after 300 parses of the
same shape at 100 levels. S: the most that warmed process reads when two thirds of its stack are first taken up by
plain recursion. "ballast": what taking up the stack costs per parse as the probe does it (finding the stack's room
by running out of it, then descending), in microseconds.

What it shows: a warmed process reads 2.2 to 5.7 times deeper than a fresh one, by construct. So no one share of the
stack makes "a warm worker read it with this share" imply "a fresh process reads it with the whole": a third is too
much for eleven of the fourteen, and the share that would hold for the worst measured here (under a sixth) rests on a
ratio that differs by construct and is not known for a construct not measured. A bound by the stack really used is
therefore no more closed than a bound by counting, costs time at every parse, and its answer depends on the process's
state; only a count of the file's own structure gives the same answer in every process. The idea is set aside.
